From be75db9af9707ca6d0bc245459906b0b8c28edc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Chatelain <ben@octop.ad>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 08:06:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] ci: Make required checks always report, and gate
 agent-authored work
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Preparation for pinning lint, test, and parity as required status checks on
main. All three filtered at the `on:` trigger, so a pull request touching none
of their paths produced no check run at all — and a required check that never
runs leaves the pull request stuck on "Expected" forever. Making them required
in that state would have deadlocked every unrelated pull request.

The filter moves into the jobs. Workflows always start; changed.sh decides
whether the expensive steps run, so a check always reports. Path lists live in
one place instead of being duplicated across triggers.

While moving them, the parity filter gains .agents/skills/**. Shared skills are
a generator input (SKILL_SOURCE), so editing one can leave generated adapters
stale, and until now that produced no parity signal on a pull request.

human-approval.yml is the gate itself. A review count cannot serve here: GitHub
disables self-approval, so required_approving_review_count on a solo repository
would deadlock every pull request. Instead an agent labels its own work
loop-authored, and the check fails until a human adds human-approved. Applying
the label is the human act; the ruleset is what makes it unskippable. Pull
requests without the loop-authored label pass straight through, so this costs
nothing on hand-written work.

Label names reach the runner through env as JSON and are never interpolated
into the script body — a label is attacker-controllable text.

The ruleset changes themselves are applied through the GitHub API, by hand,
after this merges. Doing it earlier would block every pull request on checks
that do not yet exist on main.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
